WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.930
Star Wars fans may be tough to please, but
the creator of the beloved franchise is even

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:05.930
tougher.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:09.960
According to rumor-mongering website We Got
This Covered, George Lucas wasn't just iffy

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.070
on Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker writer/director
J.J. Abrams' decision to bring back Emperor

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:19.680
Palpatine for the concluding chapter of the
Skywalker Saga, he was downright peeved.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.150
"I've got a bad feeling about this."

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:27.509
Of course, that website cites only its ever-anonymous
sources, but we have to say, this squares

00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:32.329
pretty nicely with remarks made in the past
by actor Ian McDiarmid, who has now portrayed

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:34.859
the nefarious Emperor in all three trilogies.

00:00:34.859 --> 00:00:39.510
Speaking with Digital Spy at the U.K. premiere
of Rise of Skywalker, McDiarmid indicated

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:44.000
that so far as Lucas was concerned, Palpatine
was done after being chucked down a shaft

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.960
on the exploding Death Star II at the end
of 1983's Star Wars: Return of the Jedi.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.960
He admitted:

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.000
"I thought he was dead.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.149
Because when we did Return of the Jedi, and
I was thrown down that chute to Galactic Hell,

00:00:55.149 --> 00:00:56.149
he was dead.

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:58.280
And I [asked Lucas], 'Oh, does he come back?'

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.109
And [Lucas] said, 'No, he's dead.'

00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:01.770
So I just accepted that."

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.700
He may have, but Abrams did not.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.360
According to director Colin Trevorrow, who
was initially tapped to direct Rise of Skywalker,

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:13.020
Emperor Palpatine's return was among Abrams'
first contributions to the project after he

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.420
was brought back on board to replace the Jurassic
World franchise mastermind, who departed in

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.080
late 2017.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.729
Also lending credibility to the report is
the fact that Lucas is known to have not been

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:27.270
totally psyched about Abrams' work on Star
Wars: The Force Awakens, which he felt didn't

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.430
take enough chances.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:33.700
Disney CEO Bob Iger spelled this out in his
recent memoir, The Ride of a Lifetime: Lessons

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.180
Learned From 15 Years As CEO of The Walt Disney
Company.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.270
While Iger acknowledged that it probably didn't
help that Lucas was still having some difficulty

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:45.369
at the time with having relinquished control
of his life's work, the creator's criticism

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:46.450
was pretty blunt.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.450
Iger wrote:

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.700
"Just prior to its global release, [Lucasfilm
president Kathleen Kennedy] screened The Force

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.960
Awakens for George.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.289
He didn't hide his disappointment.

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:56.090
'There's nothing new,' he said.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:00.409
In each of the films in the original trilogy,
it was important to him to present new worlds,

00:02:00.409 --> 00:02:03.329
new stories, new characters, and new technologies.

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:06.939
In this one, he said, 'There weren't enough
visual or technical leaps forward.'"

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:10.979
Lucas wasn't the only one who thought so;
The Force Awakens was criticized early and

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.050
often for essentially being a remake of Star
Wars: A New Hope.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:18.380
At times, though, it can seem like rehashing
the original trilogy is exactly what fans

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.930
wanted from the sequels; the only one of the
movies to gamble with bold narrative choices

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:27.209
is the one they most often criticize, and
it also happens to be Lucas' favorite.

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:31.870
Of all the sequel films, Lucas responded most
favorably to The Last Jedi, which was co-written

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:33.920
and directed by Rian Johnson.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.560
It's probably not a coincidence that The Last
Jedi contains the most story elements from

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.870
Lucas' original treatments for the sequels
of any of the three films, and the fewest

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.470
recycled plot points from previous movies
in the series.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.000
While Lucas has never commented on the flick
publicly, multiple outlets reported after

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.099
The Last Jedi's release that the creator had
spoken with Johnson.

00:02:52.099 --> 00:02:56.680
According to one of Lucas' representatives,
Lucas was "complementary," and told Johnson

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.780
that he thought the film was "beautifully
made."

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:02.780
The vast majority of critics felt the same
way, although The Last Jedi has proven to

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:04.860
be extremely divisive among the fandom.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.700
Those same fans, though, have also taken Rise
of Skywalker to task for being too preoccupied

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:14.250
with course-correcting many of The Last Jedi's
narrative choices and for sacrificing coherence

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.069
in the interest of cramming in overwhelming
amounts of fan service-y details.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:21.629
It's almost as if the vast majority of Star
Wars fans don't know what in the heck they

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:25.980
want, and Disney and Abrams' effort to appease
them would have fallen flat in any event.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.510
Say what you will about Lucas' prequel films;
they may have featured clunky writing, terrible

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.580
acting, and even worse CGI, but at least they
were built on a consistent vision that Lucas

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:38.470
stuck to, for better and for, well…worse.

